19.8. Expansions for x Large and a Moderate
When x>>|a|
19.8.1
U(a,x)\sim e^{-1x^2}x^{-a-1}\Big{\{}1\frac{(a+\tfrac{1}{2})(a+\tfrac{3}{2})}{2x^2}\qquad+\frac{(a+\tfrac{1}{2})(a+\tfrac{3}{2})(a+\tfrac{3}{2})}{2\cdot4x^4}\qquad\dots\Big{\}}\]
19.8.2
V(a,x)\sim\sqrt{\tfrac{2}{\pi}e^{4x^2}x^{-b}}\Big{\{}1+\frac{(a-\tfrac{1}{2})(a-\tfrac{3}{2})}{2x^2}\qquad+\frac{(a-\tfrac{1}{2})(a-\tfrac{3}{2})}{2\cdot4x^4}\qquad\dots\Big{\}}\]
These expansions form the basis for the choice of standard solutions in 19.3. The former is valid for complex x, with |arg x|<\tfrac{1}{2\pi}, in the complete sense of Watson [19.6], although valid for a wider range of |arg x| in Poincare's sense; the second series is completely valid only for x real and positive.
